/*****************************************************************************
    The drawing fields (above the bail)
*****************************************************************************/
A drawing field is spelled capital first and last (FilL, LinE; one or two letters all capitals: X, Y, UP) so that no form attribute can be it (Tony, 2026-11-12).
Form attributes keep their spelling (fill:=wall, width=400). There is NO mapping between the two: they are two vocabularies (Clod's ruling, handed over by Tony, SEQ 629).
VIRTUAL (Tony, 2026-11-12, SEQ 631): every drawing field is virtual, so a field or template that writes one gets its own copy. Addendum 2 (authors writing FilL:=wall) is RETIRED: authors never write drawing-field spellings. A FORMs field that is not virtual is still one field for everything that writes it (drawFieldsT DF-3, dfShared).
What each field is for (from the retired WorkingOn/drawing, git ede70292):
    AligN center left right, or c l r . BordeR needs an action . FilL StrokE WritE can take a colour . HeighT "does not belong here; drawing does not change the frame"
    DowN LefT RighT UP move the current point by their count . X Y set it . HomE the frame's origin . PercenT RelativE set the two modes . RadiuS for RoundeD or OvaL . EndPatH ends a path without closing
    function= and signature= name the Core Graphics call (docs/bindingmethods.md). StridE GaP DeptH are perforation's parameters.
UNPARKED (SEQ 631): PoinT, ArC and CurvE-as-a-rule are defined as rule shapes; PerfRighT PerfLefT PerfToP PerfBottoM and PerforatioN are defined. A template naming one field several times (DowN=GaP ... DowN=StridE) keeps each value, now that the fields are virtual (DF-5).
    NOT CARRIED: PoinT's action. It is written in the old drawing spellings (scope currentFRAME~, isPERCENT); its text is in git ede70292^, IncantForms/WorkingOn/drawing. Nothing draws from these entries yet: the small drawing language is still a design question (Topics/drawing).

ONE LINE: forms are kant fields laid out by setFrame and drawn by a kant walk over a short C++ floor, to a bitmap and to a PDF. 3 of 21 forms draw.

/*****************************************************************************
    Description
*****************************************************************************/
A form is a define block: a tree of fields (window, body, panels, controls) whose attributes are a little language, not assignments. Nothing fires at define.
    Layout   setFrame gives every field a frame from x y width height, down, across and space.
    Draw     the walk draws each field in the order fill, image, border, text; one floor call each.
    Event    a click selects the field, fires its action, then its attribute actions left to right; bubbling goes up the enclosing fields. Not built.
Tony is the form designer: once the attributes are fleshed out he finishes the IncantForms forms himself, using the tools.

